    {'heading': 'Lesson XXIII'},
    {'para': '**255. Declension. Comparative Adjectives.** Comparative adjectives of primary formation have a double form of stem for masculine and neuter: a stronger in ⟨yāṃs⟩ (usually ⟨īyāṃs⟩), in the strong cases, and a weaker in ⟨yas⟩ (usually ⟨īyas⟩), there being no distinction of middle and weakest cases. The voc. sing. masc. ends in ⟨yan⟩. The feminine-stem is made with ⟨ī⟩ from the weak stem-form. Thus, ⟨śreyāṃs⟩, ‘better’:'},
