    {'para': 'Fem. stem ⟨śreyasī⟩, declined like ⟨nadī⟩.'},
    {'para': '**256.** Stems in ⟨ant⟩ (or ⟨at⟩) fall into two divisions: A. those made with the suffix ⟨ant⟩ (⟨at⟩), being, with few exceptions, active participles, present and future; and B. those made with the possessive suffixes ⟨mant⟩ (or ⟨mat⟩) and ⟨vant⟩ (or ⟨vat⟩). They are masc. and neuter only, the fem. being formed with ⟨ī⟩.'},
    {'para': '**257. A. Participles in ⟨ant⟩ (or ⟨at⟩).** E. g. ⟨jīvant⟩ m., n., ‘living.’'},
    {'para': 'Masculine:'},
    {'table': {'head': ['', 'Sing.', 'Dual', 'Plural'], 'rows': [['N.V.', '⟨jīvan⟩', '⟨jīvantau⟩', '⟨jīvantas⟩'], ['A.', '⟨jīvantam⟩', '„', '⟨jīvatas⟩'], ['I.', '⟨jīvatā⟩', '⟨jīvadbhyām⟩', '⟨jīvadbhis⟩'], ['L.', '⟨jīvati⟩', '⟨jīvatos⟩', '⟨jīvatsu⟩']]}},
    {'para': 'Neuter:'},
    {'table': {'head': ['', 'Sing.', 'Dual', 'Plural'], 'rows': [['N.V.', '⟨jīvat⟩', '⟨jīvantī⟩', '⟨jīvanti⟩'], ['A.', '„', '„', '„'], ['I., L.', 'as in the masculine', '', '']]}},
    {'para': '**258.** The strong form of these participles is obtained, mechanically, by cutting off the final ⟨i⟩ from the 3rd pl. pres. (or fut.) ind. act.; thus, ⟨nayanti⟩ gives strong form of pres. act. part. ⟨nayant⟩, weak ⟨nayat⟩; ⟨tiṣṭhanti⟩, ⟨tiṣṭhant⟩ and ⟨tiṣṭhat⟩; — ⟨nahyanti⟩, ⟨nahyant⟩ and ⟨nahyat⟩; — ⟨daṇḍayanti⟩, ⟨daṇḍayant⟩ and ⟨daṇḍayat⟩; — ⟨bhaviṣyanti⟩ (fut.), ⟨bhaviṣyant⟩ and ⟨bhaviṣyat⟩.'},
    {'para': '**259.** But those verbs which in the 3rd. pl. act. lose the ⟨n⟩ of the usual ⟨nti⟩ (as e. g. the verbs following the reduplicating class in the present-system), lose it also in the present participle, and have no distinction of strong and weak stem. Thus, from ⟨hu⟩, 3rd pl. pres. ind. act. ⟨juhvati⟩, part. (only stem-form) ⟨juhvat⟩: nom.-voc. sing. masc. ⟨juhvat⟩, acc. ⟨juhvatam⟩; nom.-voc.-acc. du. ⟨juhvatau⟩, pl. ⟨juhvatas⟩; nom.-voc.-acc. sing. neut. ⟨juhvat⟩, du. ⟨juhvatī⟩, pl. ⟨juhvati⟩.∗'},
    {'para': '**260.** Only the present participles of verbs of the ⟨a⟩-class, the ⟨ya⟩-class, and causatives, invariably insert ⟨n⟩ in nom.-voc.-acc. du. neut. Present participles of the ⟨a⟩-class, of the root-class when the root ends in ⟨ā⟩, and *all* future participles, may either take or reject it; thus, neut.-sing. ⟨kirat⟩, du. ⟨kiratī⟩ or ⟨kirantī⟩; ⟨kariṣyat⟩ (fut.), du. ⟨kariṣyatī⟩ or ⟨kariṣyantī⟩; ⟨yāt⟩ (pres. part. from ⟨yā⟩, ‘go’), du. ⟨yātī⟩ or ⟨yāntī⟩. Participles of all other verbs, and all other stems in ⟨at⟩, leave out the ⟨n⟩ in the du. neut.; thus, ⟨adat⟩ (⟨ad⟩ ‘eat’, root-class), du. ⟨adatī⟩.'},
    {'para': '**261.** The adj. ⟨mahant⟩, ‘great’, takes in strong cases the stem-'},
    {'para': 'form ⟨mahānt⟩: nom.-sing. masc. ⟨mahān⟩ (see § 239, 2), acc. ⟨mahāntam⟩, voc. ⟨mahan⟩; du. neut. ⟨mahatī⟩, pl. ⟨mahānti⟩. Otherwise the inflection is like that of participles.'},
    {'para': '**262.** The feminine of participles and adjectives in ⟨ant⟩ (or ⟨at⟩) is always made with ⟨ī⟩, and the form is always identical with the nom. dual neuter.'},
    {'para': '∗ The grammarians, however, allow these verbs to insert the ⟨n⟩ in the nom.-voc.-acc. pl. neuter of the present participle.'},
